        ":question/id": "13b0b6f2-9e09-5a5a-a8f6-564d0221d479",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-342200",
        ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{2})=-2x.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(1)=-2(1)=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$"
      },
